/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   A token resolving to 69 in a leading comment opens a nested comment and
   swallows the rest of the file - see RMSTEST_56 and 57. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* Paint water onto grass. The command owns ONLY the water tiles it converts.
     Whether a beach appears on the grass NEXT to them is the question. */
  create_terrain WATER
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 3
    number_of_tiles 200
    clumping_factor 60
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_58 - is the per-command beach step GRID-WIDE or confined to the
command's own tiles?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.4, terrains.ts, Sec.15 item 28

THE QUESTION, AND IT IS NARROW
DE paints its own beach at a land and water boundary with no command asking for
it. The step runs at the end of land generation and again at the end of every
create_terrain command. That much is pinned.

What is open is the SCOPE of the per-command step. Grid-wide, as modelled, or
confined to the tiles the command actually touched. The two differ in exactly
one situation: where a command paints water beside land it never touched.

  grid-wide      -> the step looks at the whole map, finds the new land/water
                    boundary, and beaches the grass side of it.
  command-scoped -> the step only considers tiles the command owns. It owns the
                    WATER tiles. The beach belongs on the GRASS tiles beside
                    them, which the command never touched, so no beach appears.

METHOD
An all-grass map with no water anywhere, then one command converting grass to
water in a few compact blobs. Every water tile on the finished map came from
that command, and every grass tile beside it did not.

  BEACH PRESENT around the new water -> GRID-WIDE. The shipped model is right,
                                        item 28 closes, nothing changes.
  NO BEACH at all                    -> COMMAND-SCOPED. terrains.ts beaches
                                        coastline the engine leaves bare, and
                                        the per-command step needs a tile mask.

There is no third outcome to worry about: the map contains no other land/water
boundary, so any beach at all can only have come from this one.

WHY THE MAP CARRIES NO WATER OF ITS OWN
Deliberate, and it is the whole design. If the base terrain were water, the
land-generation beach step would have already run and laid a waterline, and a
beach found afterwards could not be attributed to the per-command step. This map
gives the per-command step the only boundary on it.

Clumping is high and the clump count low so the blobs are compact and their
edges are easy to read. Nothing here uses forest or leaves, which auto-place
objects, or shallows, which item 29 has just shown are edged on one side only.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWO RUNS.

Two rather than one because a beach could in principle appear on some boundaries
and not others - a blob that happens to touch the map edge behaves differently
from one in open grass - and a single generation cannot show that.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches BEACH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches WATER
  Beach tile count is the answer. Zero is command-scoped; a ring roughly
  proportional to the water perimeter is grid-wide.

  If beach appears on SOME blobs and not others, do not average it - record
  which blobs and look for what distinguishes them. That is a third model and it
  would mean the step is neither of the two above.
*/
